---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { DISTRIBUTIONS, INSTALL_COMMAND, RELEASE, REPO, type Level } from '../data/site';

const levels: Record<Level, string> = {
  daily: 'Main setup',
  vm: 'Tested',
  built: 'Builds only',
};
---

<Page
  title="Ngó Sen: a Vietnamese input method for Linux"
  description="Ngó Sen is a Vietnamese input method for fcitx5 on Linux. One-line install, and an honest list of where it has been tested."
  heading="Ngó Sen"
  lang="en"
>
  <p slot="lede">
    A Vietnamese input method optimized for Linux, running on fcitx5. No root daemon, no underlined
    preedit: one typing mode, Gõ Sen, for every app.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ngó Sen means “lotus stem”. Bugs can be reported in
    <a href={`${REPO}/issues`}>Issues</a>. The rest of this site is in Vietnamese.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <pre tabindex="0"><code>{INSTALL_COMMAND}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The script picks the prebuilt package for your distribution from the latest GitHub release, asks
    before it downloads the package, checks the file against <code>SHA256SUMS</code> and installs it
    with your package manager. It replaces <code>fcitx5-lotus</code> if that is installed; your
    configuration is kept. Its prompts are in Vietnamese: answer <kbd>c</kbd> to continue. The source is
    <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/install.sh`}>install.sh</a>.
  </p>
  <p>Then:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Restart fcitx5 with <code>fcitx5 -rd</code>. Do this again after every update.</li>
    <li>Open “Fcitx5 Configuration” and add the input method named Ngó Sen.</li>
    <li>On KDE Wayland, select “Fcitx 5” under System Settings, Virtual Keyboard.</li>
    <li>
      In the Ngó Sen settings window, pick <strong>Gõ Sen</strong> as the default mode.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    On Ubuntu 26.04, log out and back in after installing, then run
    <code>gnome-extensions enable forward-keys@ngosen.github.io</code> once. The extension works around a
    GNOME bug that drops the backspaces the input method sends.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tested">Where it has been tested</h2>
  <p>
    Packages exist for these distributions on x86_64. A package that builds is not a package that works,
    so each one is labelled by how far it was tested, as listed in the notes of release
    {RELEASE.version}.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Distribution</th>
          <th scope="col">Tested level</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          DISTRIBUTIONS.map((item) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{item.name}</th>
              <td>
                <span class="stamp" data-level={item.level}>
                  {levels[item.level]}
                </span>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    “Main setup” means the maintainer types on that system every day, on KDE Plasma Wayland. “Tested”
    means a handful of apps were tried in a virtual machine: CachyOS with Hyprland, Ubuntu 26.04 with
    GNOME Wayland, Linux Mint 22 for Ubuntu 24.04 and MX 25 for Debian 13; the two Ubuntu tests used a
    build from source. “Builds only” means the package builds and passes the test suite at build time;
    nobody has typed with it there.
  </p>

  <h2 id="differences">What differs from fcitx5-lotus</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      No background server. Old text is deleted with keys forwarded through fcitx5, or through XTEST on
      X11, so nothing runs with special device permissions.
    </li>
    <li>
      Two typing modes: Gõ Sen, for fast typing, browsers, Electron apps and terminals, and Preedit,
      plus an Emoji mode. Old Uinput and Surrounding Text settings move to Gõ Sen on their own.
    </li>
    <li>
      The Bamboo composition core is written in Rust in the Fedora, Arch, openSUSE and Ubuntu 24.04+
      packages. Debian 12, 13 and Ubuntu 22.04 keep the Go core; both type the same text.
    </li>
    <li>
      Ngó Sen numbers its own releases from 0.5.0. Packages carry an epoch, so package managers still
      treat 0.5.0 as newer than 3.5.10.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not fixed: SDL games outside X11 ignore forwarded keys and get text without tone marks, and the snap
    build of VS Code types through XIM, where about one sentence in ten gets a wrong tone mark. Telex is
    the method used and checked daily; VNI works but has not been checked closely.
  </p>
  <p>
    Planned next: an IBus build for GNOME and Ubuntu (package <code>ibus-ngosen</code>), and a
    build that runs directly on wlroots compositors such as Sway, Hyprland, river, labwc and Wayfire,
    without fcitx5. Source, releases and the changelog are at
    <a href={REPO}>github.com/ngosen/ngosen</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
